  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "What is the legislature?",
      "a": "The legislature is the part of government that makes laws, such as a parliament or congress. Its members are usually elected by the people.",
      "asks": [
        "Who makes the laws?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the executive?",
      "a": "The executive runs the country day to day. It is led by a president or prime minister, with ministers in charge of areas like health and education.",
      "asks": [
        "Who runs the government?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What is the judiciary?",
      "a": "The judiciary is the system of courts and judges. It decides cases, explains the law and checks that the other branches follow the constitution.",
      "asks": [
        "What do courts do?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why split power into three branches?",
      "a": "So no single person or group has all the power. Each branch can check the others, which helps protect people's rights.",
      "asks": [
        "What is separation of powers?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Is the prime minister part of parliament?",
      "a": "In parliamentary systems like the UK, India and Japan, yes: the prime minister sits in parliament. In presidential systems like the USA and Brazil, the president is separate from Congress.",
      "asks": [
